+/// Gives `[at, at + len)` write access through `VirtualProtect`, the call the
+/// system's books follow, so that the mapping the host faults on and the
+/// protection the system reports for the title's address say the same thing.
+///
+/// `mach_vm_remap` takes the protection (and the maximum protection) of the
+/// mapping it copies from, while what the system reports for the title's
+/// address is what was set there before it: the two part. A page guarded
+/// read-only before its buffer was aliased goes on reading as read-only while
+/// writes to it land in the buffer unseen by the trackers, and a page aliased
+/// onto a mapping that may not be written faults in the host while the books
